// Building a Huffman decoder from nothing but code lengths.
//
// RFC 1951 section 3.2.2: a DEFLATE stream never transmits the codes, only
// how many bits each symbol's code has. The codes follow from that, because
// the canonical construction fixes them -- shorter codes first, and within a
// length, ascending by symbol. So a decoder needs exactly two things: how many
// codes there are of each length, and the symbols in the order the codes run.
//
// A tree is those two lists, returned as an `int[][]` of [counts, symbols],
// because `id` has no struct and a pair is the smallest thing that is not one.
// counts has 16 cells (a DEFLATE code is at most 15 bits, and cell 0 counts
// the symbols that do not appear); symbols has one cell per symbol, of which
// only the coded ones are ever read.

// The pair has to be grown one cell at a time and filled afterwards: a list
// literal cannot appear in a return clause, and `[tab, row]` after `int[][]`
// parses as a third dimension rather than as a value.
inf_tree(int[] xs) {
  int[][] rows = [];
  push(rows, []);
  inf_grow(xs, rows);
} return int[][] rows;

// Splitting the sizing across two functions is the action limit talking: the
// two pushes, the two sizings and the fill are five statements and no two of
// them can be merged without hiding one inside the other.
inf_grow(int[] xs, int[][] rows) {
  push(rows, []);
  lst_fill(rows[0], 16, 0);
  inf_fill(xs, rows[0], rows[1]);
} return void;

// The symbol list is sized to the alphabet rather than to the number of coded
// symbols, which wastes a few cells and saves a pass counting them.
inf_fill(int[] xs, int[] tab, int[] row) {
  int len_v = len(xs);
  lst_fill(row, len_v, 0);
  inf_fill2(xs, tab, row);
} return void;
